# Command claude-log-view

## What does this command do:

Python curses TUI for browsing and viewing `myclaude` session logs. Picks the
`_<REL>` cwd-group matching the current directory (or falls back to the most
recently modified group), lists its `*.log` files, and views the selected log
either raw (`less -R`) or cleaned through an ANSI-stripping pipeline
(`ansifilter`/`ansi2txt` | `col -b` | `tr -d '\r'` | `cat -s` | `less`).

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: git clone git@github.com:kevinpinscoe/tools.git ~/tools (then keep ~/tools on PATH)
For Raspberry Pi install using: git clone git@github.com:kevinpinscoe/tools.git ~/tools (then keep ~/tools on PATH)
For Mac install using: git clone git@github.com:kevinpinscoe/tools.git ~/tools (then keep ~/tools on PATH)
For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using: git clone git@github.com:kevinpinscoe/tools.git ~/tools (then keep ~/tools on PATH)

> Not a package — it's a script in the `kevinpinscoe/tools` repo. Pull to sync
> updates: `git -C ~/tools pull`. Needs only `python3` (stdlib) and `less`;
> `ansifilter` or `ansi2txt` is optional but required for the cleaned view.

## Command path

For Fedora run: ~/tools/claude-log-view
For Raspberry Pi run: ~/tools/claude-log-view
For Mac run: ~/tools/claude-log-view
For Mac container run: ~/tools/claude-log-view

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at ~/tools/RUNBOOK.md (## claude-log-view section)

## Command options

# Open the picker (starts on the cwd's log group)
claude-log-view

# Inside the picker:
#   j / k / g / G / PgUp / PgDn   navigate the list
#   Enter                          drill into a cwd-group, or open a log
#   d                               back to the cwd-group list
#   r                               toggle raw <-> cleaned view of the open log
#   q / Esc                         quit (or go up one level)
